WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.920
A counterexample is one case that breaks a

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:04.010
claim about everything.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.580
A proof by contradiction starts with a supposition and

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.950
follows what it would imply.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.270
If it leads to two facts that cannot both hold,

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.770
the supposition fails.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.510
Competitions often ask whether something is possible.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.430
If it is, show it.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.300
If it is not, you need an argument, and contradiction is

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.870
the standard way to build one.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.920
A student says: multiplying always makes

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:28.730
a number bigger.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.450
Five times three is fifteen, which is bigger than five.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:33.830
Is the claim true?

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.450
Pause and hunt for a case that breaks it.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.660
Six times one half is three, which is smaller.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.000
Six times one is still six.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:46.820
Six times zero is zero.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.450
Any one of these is a counterexample,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:50.520
so the claim is false.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.540
A counterexample must obey the claim's own conditions.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.370
If the claim were about multiplying a positive number by

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.310
a number greater than one, then a half and zero would

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.120
not be allowed, and the repaired claim would be true.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.490
Failed claims often become true once the conditions

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:08.600
are stated properly.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:10.550
Now a contradiction.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.870
Nine coins all show heads.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.490
Each move flips exactly two coins.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.230
Can all nine show tails?

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.930
Suppose it could be done.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.730
Each move changes the number of heads by two,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:22.790
or not at all.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.800
So the number of heads stays odd for ever.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.330
It starts at nine, which is odd.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.530
All tails would mean zero heads, which is even.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.570
An odd number can never become zero this way.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.490
Our supposition has led to something impossible,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.730
so the task cannot be done.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.040
A counterexample is one allowed case that

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:43.870
breaks a claim.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.440
A contradiction supposes something is possible and shows

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.670
that leads to nonsense.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.170
Notebook challenge: seven cups stand upright.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.410
Each move turns over exactly two cups.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.850
Can all seven end upside down?
